ice noodle roll","dish/rice-noodle-roll"],["Siu mei","dish/siu-mei"],["Sweet and sour pork","dish/sweet-and-sour-pork"],["Turnip cake","dish/turnip-cake"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/a/a5/Xiajiao.jpg/500px-Xiajiao.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"sfllaw of Flickr","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Xiajiao.jpg"}},"dish/neera":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/neera","n":"Neera","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Indonesian cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Neera, also called palm nectar, is a sap extracted from the inflorescence of various species of toddy palms and used as a drink.  Neera extraction is generally performed before sunrise. It is sweet, translucent in colour. It is susceptible to natural fermentation at ambient temperature within a few hours of extraction, and is also known as palm wine. Once fermented, Neera becomes toddy. Neera is widely consumed in India, Sri Lanka, Africa, Malaysia, Indonesia, Thailand, and Myanmar. Neera is not the juice made from palm fruit."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-neera"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neera"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":1,"links":[["Borassus flabellifer","ingredient/borassus-flabellifer"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Daily Cooking Quest","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"dailycookingquest.com","note":"Indonesian home cook Anita's blog with a large archive of Indonesian recipes.","url":"https://dailycookingquest.com/cuisines/indonesian.html","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Indonesian cuisine","links":[["Arem-arem","dish/arem-arem"],["Arsik","dish/arsik"],["Asam pedas","dish/asam-pedas"],["Ayam goreng","dish/ayam-goreng"],["Ayam goreng kalasan","dish/ayam-goreng-kalasan"],["Ayam panggang","dish/ayam-panggang"],["Ayam Taliwang","dish/ayam-taliwang"],["Bajigur","dish/bajigur"],["Bakakak hayam","dish/bakakak-hayam"],["Bakpia","dish/bakpia"],["Bakwan","dish/bakwan"],["Bekasang","dish/bekasang"],["Betutu","dish/betutu"],["Bika ambon","dish/bika-ambon"],["Binte biluhuta","dish/binte-biluhuta"],["Bir pletok","dish/bir-pletok"],["Bolu kukus","dish/bolu-kukus"],["Botok","dish/botok"],["Brem","dish/brem"],["Bubur kacang hijau","dish/bubur-kacang-hijau"],["Bubur ketan hitam","dish/bubur-ketan-hitam"],["Bubur pedas","dish/bubur-pedas"],["Buntil","dish/buntil"],["Burgo (food)","dish/burgo-food"],["Cendol","dish/cendol"],["Cenil","dish/cenil"],["Cilok","dish/cilok"],["Cimpa","dish/cimpa"],["Corn fritter","dish/corn-fritter"],["Coto makassar","dish/coto-makassar"],["Dadar gulung","dish/dadar-gulung"],["Dadiah","dish/dadiah"],["Dengke mas naniura","dish/dengke-mas-naniura"],["Empal gentong","dish/empal-gentong"],["Emping","dish/emping"],["Es buah","dish/es-buah"],["Es doger","dish/es-doger"],["Es goyobod","dish/es-goyobod"],["Gado-gado","dish/gado-gado"],["Geplak","dish/geplak"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/d/d8/Neera_manufactured_from_Kollam_Kaippuzha_Plant%2C_Feb_2016.jpg/500px-Neera_manufactured_from_Kollam_Kaippuzha_Plant%2C_Feb_2016.jpg","w":500,"h":373,"by":"Arunvrparavur","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Neera_manufactured_from_Kollam_Kaippuzha_Plant%2C_Feb_2016.jpg"}},"ingredient/brassica":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/brassica","n":"Brassica","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 2 dishes across 4 cuisines","intro":[["Brassica () is a genus of plants in the cabbage and mustard family (Brassicaceae). The members of the genus are informally known as cruciferous vegetables, cabbages, mustard plants, or simply brassicas. Crops from this genus are sometimes called cole crops—derived from the Latin caulis, denoting the stem or stalk of a plant."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-brassica"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brassica"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":4,"groups":[["Georgian cuisine","cuisine/georgian-cuisine",[["Pkhali","dish/pkhali"]]],["Kazakh cuisine","cuisine/kazakh-cuisine",[["Orama (dish)","dish/orama-dish"]]],["Kyrgyz cuisine","cuisine/kyrgyz-cuisine",[["Orama (dish)","dish/orama-dish"]]],["Uzbek cuisine","cuisine/uzbek-cuisine",[["Orama (dish)","dish/orama-dish"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Animal fat","ingredient/animal-fat"],["Cucurbita","ingredient/cucurbita"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"],["Pumpkin","ingredient/pumpkin"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Beta","ingredient/beta"],["Eggplant","ingredient/eggplant"],["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Juglans regia","ingredient/juglans-regia"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Phaseolus","ingredient/phaseolus"],["Spinacia","ingredient/spinacia"],["Vinegar","ingredient/vinegar"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/d/d9/Brassica_rapa_plant.jpg/500px-Brassica_rapa_plant.jpg","w":500,"h":667,"by":"TeunSpaans","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brassica_rapa_plant.jpg"}},"ingredient/presunto":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/presunto","n":"Presunto","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Presunto is dry-cured ham from Portugal, similar to Italian prosciutto or Spanish jamón. Among the wide variety of presuntos in Portugal, the best known are presunto from Chaves, produced in the north of Portugal, and from the Alentejo, in the south, made from local Alentejano pigs."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-presunto"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Presunto"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Portuguese cuisine","cuisine/portuguese-cuisine",[["Pudim Abade de Priscos","dish/pudim-abade-de-priscos"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Port Wine","ingredient/port-wine"],["Port wine","ingredient/port-wine"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/4/40/Presunto_de_Chaves.jpg/500px-Presunto_de_Chaves.jpg","w":500,"h":334,"by":"I, Carlos Botelho","lic":"CC BY 2.5","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.5","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Presunto_de_Chaves.jpg"}},"ingredient/prosciutto":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/prosciutto","n":"Prosciutto","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Prosciutto, also known as prosciutto crudo, is an uncooked, unsmoked, and dry-cured ham. It is usually served thinly sliced."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-prosciutto"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prosciutto"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine",[["Saltimbocca","dish/saltimbocca"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Salvia","ingredient/salvia"],["Salvia officinalis","ingredient/salvia-officinalis"],["Veal","ingredient/veal"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/1/15/Prosciutto_di_Parma%2C_ham_producing.jpg/500px-Prosciutto_di_Parma%2C_ham_producing.jpg","w":500,"h":667,"by":"Udo Schröter","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prosciutto_di_Parma%2C_ham_producing.jpg"}},"ingredient/kheer-bengali-sweets":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/kheer-bengali-sweets","n":"Kheer (Bengali sweets)","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Kheer or Meoa is a sweet from the Bengal region of the Indian subcontinent. It is not only a sweet by itself, but it is also used as the main ingredient of many other sweets. In North India, Kheer (Payesam) is a type of rice pudding. But in Bengal, in the same spelling and sound, Kheer is a completely different dish. It is very similar to the Khoa but with its own distinct flavor and texture. Kheer, a type of evaporated milk, is primarily made using cow or buffalo milk. The process involves boiling pure milk for an extended period, typically over an hour, to reduce it to one-third of its original volume. This concentrated form of milk is known as Kheer. Sometimes, ingredients like sugar, arrowroot, or Suji (semolina) are added during boiling to enhance sweetness and create a different flavor profile. However, the traditional version of Kheer, without these additions, is"]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-kheer-bengali-sweets"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kheer_(Bengali_sweets)"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Bengali cuisine","cuisine/bengali-cuisine",[["Sarbhaja","dish/sarbhaja"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Cardamom","ingredient/cardamom"],["Ghee","ingredient/ghee"],["Maida (flour)","ingredient/maida-flour"],["Milk skin","ingredient/milk-skin"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/c/c1/Taler_Kheer.jpg/500px-Taler_Kheer.jpg","w":500,"h":340,"by":"Sumit Surai","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Taler_Kheer.jpg"}}}